# Integration Basics: Accumulating Change

> Differentiation slices change infinitely fine; integration glues
> the slices back together. As anti-differentiation it recovers a
> function; as area-under-the-curve it totals change itself.

## 1. Two Faces of Integration

**Integration** is the reverse of differentiation: if the derivative
of F is f, then an **antiderivative** of f is F. But integration is
also **accumulation**: add up many thin rectangles under a curve
(a **Riemann sum**) and, as the slices shrink, their total tends to
the exact area — the two ideas coincide.

@math label="eq:integral-def"
\int f(x)\,dx = F(x) + C \quad \text{where} \quad F'(x) = f(x)
@/math

The elongated S is the integral sign; f(x) dx are the thin slices
being summed; the dx names the slicing variable.

## 2. Indefinite vs Definite

| Feature | Indefinite integral | Definite integral |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Answer | a family of functions | a number |
| Bounds | none | lower and upper, e.g. 0 to 2 |
| + C? | required | cancels out |
| Meaning | anti-derivative | signed area under the curve |

## 3. The Power Rule

@math label="eq:power-rule"
\int x^n \, dx = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C \quad (n \neq -1)
\qquad\qquad
\int \frac{1}{x}\,dx = \ln|x| + C
@/math

Raise the power by one, divide by the new power. The exceptional
n = −1 would divide by zero — its answer is the natural logarithm,
which is exactly the function whose derivative is 1/x.

[example]
- ∫ x² dx = x³/3 + C (power 2 → 3, divide by 3).
- ∫ (3x² + 4x − 5) dx = 3·x³/3 + 4·x²/2 − 5x + C = x³ + 2x² − 5x + C,
  integrating term by term (the **sum rule**) with constants pulled
  through (the **constant multiple rule**).
[/example]

## 4. Why the + C Matters

Infinitely many functions share a derivative: x² + 1, x² − 7 and
x² + π all differentiate to 2x. Integration only recovers the shape,
so every answer carries **+ C** — the family of vertical translates
of one curve. Forgetting + C loses solutions: any particle with
velocity 2t has position t² + C, and only an initial condition picks
out one member of the family.

## 5. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus

[definition]
**Fundamental theorem of calculus:** if F is any antiderivative of a
continuous f, then the definite integral of f from a to b equals
F(b) − F(a). No + C appears, because it cancels in the subtraction.
[/definition]

[example]
Evaluate the definite integral of x² from 0 to 2.
- Antiderivative: F(x) = x³/3.
- Apply the theorem: F(2) − F(0) = 8/3 − 0 = **8/3**.
- Geometrically: the area under y = x² between x = 0 and x = 2 is
  exactly 8/3 ≈ 2.67 square units.
[/example]

## 6. From Velocity to Distance

If v(t) is velocity, its integral over a time interval is the
**displacement** — signed, so backwards motion subtracts. The
**distance travelled** integrates the speed, which is |v(t)|, keeping
every contribution positive.

[example]
A particle moves with v(t) = 3t². Over the interval from t = 0 to
t = 2 its displacement is ∫ 3t² dt evaluated at the bounds = 8 − 0
= 8 units. Since v(t) never turns negative here, the distance
travelled also equals 8.
[/example]

## 7. What Integration Computes

| Situation | Integrate | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| area between curve and x-axis | y = f(x) | signed area |
| velocity known | v(t) | displacement (or distance) |
| marginal cost known (economics) | marginal function | total cost |
| flow rate known | rate of filling | total volume |
| population growth rate known | growth rate | total change in population |

Any "total from a rate" question is an integral in disguise —
integration is the mathematics of adding up continuous change.

## 8. A Peek at Techniques

[example]
Substitution — the chain rule in reverse. Evaluate ∫ 2x(x² + 1)³ dx.
- Let u = x² + 1, so du = 2x dx — the whole integrand's awkward part.
- The integral becomes ∫ u³ du = u⁴/4 + C = (x² + 1)⁴/4 + C.
- Check by differentiating: the chain rule returns 2x(x² + 1)³. ✓
[/example]

Products of unlike functions (x·sin x, x·eˣ) need **integration by
parts** — moving the derivative from one factor to the other. When
no closed form exists (e.g. e^(−x²)), numerical methods such as the
trapezium rule approximate the area to any accuracy a real problem
demands — which is exactly how calculators evaluate integrals.

[note]
Areas below the x-axis count as negative in a definite integral; to
measure geometric area, split the interval where the curve crosses
the axis and integrate each piece separately.
[/note]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| ∫ x² dx? | x³/3 + C |
| ∫ 1/x dx? | ln ∣x∣ + C |
| ∫ (3x² + 4x − 5) dx? | x³ + 2x² − 5x + C |
| Definite integral of x² from 0 to 2? | 8/3 |
| Why + C? | derivatives cannot see vertical shifts |
| ∫ 2x(x² + 1)³ dx? | (x² + 1)⁴/4 + C |
| Velocity gives what when integrated? | displacement (|v| gives distance) |
